#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230002} :activity/id #uuid "bb024668-aa54-5547-86a8-56f97596b95b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048231 :topic/id #uuid "8a6eb2f8-5287-523f-8df7-f21c0db8796c" :topic/math-academy-id 6690} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230188 :tutorial/content "Matrix exponentials can be used for solving homogeneous systems of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix.\n\nFor example, consider the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/f9/f94140f8c9fe736ca7c574547658856e85d938eb08064046716be8eb1a2748de.svg)\nwhere ![](images/26/26dcf2c367c915460d09f4f38b40e4f792a06b6ab44291b4739a6935529daf89.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/90/9000e03ddf617a1e3c4e0eed5da2b846d62e3d68588b601d90643783960d17df.svg)\n\nRecall that given the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi(t) \\, \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is some constant vector.\n\nSince we have already seen how to compute the standard fundamental matrix, we will not repeat the calculation here. So, computing the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system, we get\n![](images/11/11e0da79656b7f8e2c84c6c54e6dd6b54cf53cbb8e746d44dfb13329c3d2ed8f.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\n**Note:** Recall that for the general solution of the ordinary differential equation $x'(t)=ax(t),$ we have \n\n$$\nx'(t)=ax(t)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nx(t) = c \\, e^{at}, \n\\quad\nc \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nNow, for the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t),$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c},\n\\quad\n\\mathbf{c} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "978fc2f4-910c-5bef-9ae3-3da9377642ce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14515 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c774d65b-c500-58e4-aa9a-e222d3d136ae" :step/math-academy-id 592270 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230003}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229142 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228910 :question/id #uuid "26df399d-cd87-5b5d-9f3f-7ced4ca5fd06" :question/math-academy-id "e-21707"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5bc609b-dc5b-52e9-9f0e-206f32bb829b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050228}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445613}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Homogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"} :step/id #uuid "d3b52974-38d7-50fa-b648-b51510a9a617" :step/math-academy-id 592271 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230004}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230193 :tutorial/content "Now, let's find the solution to the initial value problem below\n![](images/31/3111f065d441881e050417b345b34947509f175f00ebd5402963e432d013348e.svg)\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/5c/5c4ed0a2e9db0979e4fc0342c2e4db7c23c60cf58293a496fa661ff1c03fccde.svg)\nwhere ![](images/71/7164659247c5290544655b05943d7d998f67cb1cc3b4cc45bab0902e8059bc75.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/e3/e3f94e8ce2cc2063d469fbac2af87232cc130f788243c1507bfe9972dd4b04c1.svg)\n\nRecall that the general solution of the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Phi_0(t) = e^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is some constant vector.\n\nEvaluating both sides of $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\mathbf{c}$ at $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c \\\\ x(0) & ={e}^{A(0)}\\,c \\\\ x(0) & =I\\,c \\\\ c & =x(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to our initial value problem is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\Phi_0(t) \\, \\mathbf{x}(0).\n$$\n\nComputing the standard fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ of the system, we get\n![](images/fc/fc4e1ba16be420fbe5e53e2dbb4ee6da96c7097bbe4e117315407ec4dc65365f.svg)\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} & t{e}^{7t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(3-2t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -2{e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9cb11887-9535-5a54-a180-eb20a7534696" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14529 :tutorial/title "Solving Initial Value Problems"} :step/id #uuid "5206e604-fe74-5b8a-800c-cdb747dc1e28" :step/math-academy-id 592274 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229143 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228918 :question/id #uuid "ead811e0-8551-5b15-8b49-711cbe0b8498" :question/math-academy-id "e-21725"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d611c75-818f-508f-a7a4-49edb32baa41" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229657} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229682} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482598}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential"} :step/id #uuid "c93fbf09-3c42-5a66-8e76-1cf0d810f44a" :step/math-academy-id 592275 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230006}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230006 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230194 :tutorial/content "Next, let's find the solution to the initial value problem \n![](images/93/93f9c58b8a9cea64ba97f43f2831336ea8d38a6fd6165211a54d2822415c1497.svg)\ngiven that ![](images/ee/ee25228bf71d1b7d9d0688af06a7f1335c252310127b403ed0ed37f7fe277c07.svg) where ![](images/3a/3a3ac9326741c0e2390a8e2f079808f0b432b740149ef4b034ffa7568dc33848.svg)\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n![](images/2b/2b81cf358ffb3df49f327b63138640ac452a249f989353b521be34d531aeffd9.svg)\nwhere ![](images/11/1110f0bdbe784b21683bfb9610ca9c04e5a45755954d49a15124e530feef2002.svg) So, the matrix of the system is ![](images/47/47d8a0214e5ccdf6625a330d23003dec11cbc3a4f35cb2992df29a688963e4b2.svg)\n\nGiven the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution is $\\mathbf{x}(t) = e^{At} \\, \\mathbf{c}.$ We can determine the constant vector $\\mathbf{c}$ using the initial condition at time $t=t_0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = e^{At_0} \\, \\mathbf{c} \\implies \\mathbf{c} = e^{-At_0} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0),\n$$\n\nsince the inverse of $e^{At_0}$ is $e^{-At_0}.$ Plugging this back into our general solution gives the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our specific problem (with $t_0=1$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7(t-1)) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0fe9a975-9d92-5f79-8a67-34af55196fb3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14530 :tutorial/title "Solving Initial Value Problems With Shifted Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "19b34f7c-755c-5e1b-a16f-22bd17d1ecc7" :step/math-academy-id 592278 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229144 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228917 :question/id #uuid "b061f2b1-b015-582f-921f-9b444db631ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-21723"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e58bb6-6d90-5171-bba2-6ffa5a0c91d9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482582}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem Using a Given Matrix Exponential: Shifted Initial Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "633a401e-5116-5c9e-a0b4-8848cf596d7b" :step/math-academy-id 592279 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230008}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230008 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230192 :tutorial/content "Before proceeding with the next example, let's recall some important properties of matrix exponentials:\n\n1\\. *Law of Exponents:* If $A$ is a constant square matrix, then\n\n  $$\n  e^{A(t+s)} = e^{At}\\,e^{As}.\n  $$\n\n2\\. *Inverse:* For any $t,$ the matrix $e^{At}$ is invertible and \n\n  $$\n  (e^{At})^{-1} = e^{-At}.\n  $$\n\n3\\. *Identity:* For any square matrix $A,$\n\n  $$\n  e^{A\\cdot 0} = I.\n  $$\n\n4\\. *Product of exponentials:* If $A$ and $B$ commute (i.e., $AB = BA$), then\n\n  $$\n  e^{A}e^{B} = e^{A+B}.\n  $$\n\n  **Watch out!** In general, this identity is *not true* unless $A$ and $B$ commute.\n\nLet's see how these properties can be used in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ae3cfa7-a5a5-5a50-aefa-8b8dec561567" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14528 :tutorial/title "Reminder: Properties of Matrix Exponentials"} :step/id #uuid "eb56047e-3845-5f8e-9282-d41f308270e3" :step/math-academy-id 592282 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230009}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230009 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229145 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228911 :question/id #uuid "ace067cd-e1d0-5b3e-bae2-bf58dcac3585" :question/math-academy-id "e-21711"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ff837c0-0ebb-5a9e-acb4-894261e0318c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482614}] :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution: Homogeneous Systems"} :step/id #uuid "6a90e198-ec80-5daf-8cb9-69841a16a32d" :step/math-academy-id 592283}] :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs Using Matrix Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229256}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482627} :activity/id #uuid "14357ccb-7387-5d90-95d5-9ec0c77cd805" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048231 :topic/id #uuid "8a6eb2f8-5287-523f-8df7-f21c0db8796c" :topic/math-academy-id 6690} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9df7eb-dd1e-53e0-a87d-68b6fd1f480c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481800} {:answer/id #uuid "b3959273-da78-54bd-9652-5e91ff910d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482197} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf2cf7c-d4c9-58ad-ac1c-f6f73d55b4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482226} {:answer/id #uuid "c6299321-746c-562a-a610-1a2b40cd8433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482243} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfaca09-e572-55e6-9c7f-bc94a914bffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482226} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf4268c8-502e-5529-b80f-13d26b351b38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d66735c3-ba98-5f1c-8e1d-7d80133c59cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-335266" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(9t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(9t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2379b8ac-5990-5a88-876d-7a3b13ce4148" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09fc895b-be35-574c-a0f1-d2e9dbdc5824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481790} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed34351-e7a9-51f4-906b-adec1b5a834b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "properties of the determinant" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481812} {:answer/id #uuid "96028a64-5f62-51b8-b0ec-6376058e920e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482128} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b37ee0-60b6-54bb-88d3-5a9b8ced5669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "definition of a solution curve" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481790} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c1a9e2c-69c2-57dc-98a4-cfafabe3fc1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482403} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7155f794-f353-5a0c-8911-a1f285b25e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "computing $det({Φ}_{0}(0))$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482037} {:answer/id #uuid "820a69e5-51c6-5ddf-9a50-13e477a4befa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "differentiating at $t=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482074} {:answer/id #uuid "8b33370f-a59f-59ec-a300-6d743e8570be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "using the initial condition ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482100} {:answer/id #uuid "e9aa91ff-5e69-576a-ba8f-43568cbef52e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "applying the chain rule to ${Φ}_{0}(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482333}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482100} :answer-field/id #uuid "31d8bd8e-b3aa-594c-88a8-b125c8eb1696" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482405} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11948125-54a4-5bb6-9128-a9f6fecd3d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "replacing ${Φ}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481821} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b3c268-1dd4-5950-ab2e-09770223774b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "evaluating both sides at $t=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482318} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e2076e-2a67-59cb-8006-dae1d2be288f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "applying the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482320} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f930bb-5307-59dd-80a7-b9bdb0cade2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "integrating $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481821} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6570167-3f96-5d88-b259-3613309f60d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1163c64f-8eb6-5387-bd44-2f1d21a98032" :question/math-academy-id "q-336479" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(0)=c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,x(0)$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ is obtained from the defining initial value problem for ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ by  \n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by  \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is a fundamental matrix for the system, every solution can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c\\,\\text{for some constant vector }c.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{properties of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is defined as the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nThe second condition, ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},$ is exactly line $\\text{L4}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L4}$ is obtained from the defining initial value problem for ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ by $\\text{using the initial condition }{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L4},$ and $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c,\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L4}$ we know ${Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.$ Replacing ${Φ}_{0}(0)$ with ${I}_{n}$ in the equation $x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c$ gives\n\n$$\nx(0)={I}_{n}c=c,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly line $\\text{L5}.$ Thus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{replacing }{Φ}_{0}(0)\\text{ with }{I}_{n}\\text{ in }x(0)={Φ}_{0}(0)c.$"} :step/id #uuid "28f5bdee-7eed-595c-9820-abd9f0486f9a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482640}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61796693-027e-5e34-ac41-7245117c63ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the definition of the matrix exponential" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481998} {:answer/id #uuid "67302da8-f87a-5742-b68d-b6432fc52247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the identity ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{XY}$ for matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482012} {:answer/id #uuid "aecba564-d67f-5fda-a785-7112b2e9e6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "the fact that ${e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}$ when $X$ and $Y$ commute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482187}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482187} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad0248d-955a-5ee8-87b5-a6a9266226fa" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482409} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba11d7e-2a61-553a-9f1e-d3dc6f191266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481924} {:answer/id #uuid "887d453d-74c3-5783-9f8d-da874648d69f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}=I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482090} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd63aad-1f80-570f-9351-385e1f17ff12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{A{t}_{0}}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}=-I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482144}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481924} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f300979-2c03-5bca-8541-1101107fdf73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482463} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16aae11e-7722-5d5d-a3ff-56d6a8a48158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "evaluating line $\\text{L3}$ at $t={t}_{0}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481838} {:answer/id #uuid "1edc9f2b-095b-537f-8b55-2d74f8563acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "substituting $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ into $x(t)={e}^{At}c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481858} {:answer/id #uuid "619ca3f7-48eb-583d-ab51-495c74c6bef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "multiplying line $\\text{L3}$ on the right by ${e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481858} :answer-field/id #uuid "b775a29d-3fe5-5512-9ef6-783e154724b1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f598b188-bddf-5e21-881f-0c355a5c9889" :question/math-academy-id "q-337119" :question/problem "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$  \n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L6}$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ Line $\\text{L5}$ gives\n\n$$\n({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c.\n$$\n\nSince $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}={I}_{n}$, the right-hand side simplifies to $c$, so\n\n$$\nc=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nSimplify once, then apply $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}$, to obtain\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L6}.$ Therefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by the fact that $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$, $\\text{L6}$, and $\\text{L7}.$ From $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\text{L6}$, we substitute $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})$ to obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L6}$ by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L7}$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Since $At$ and $-A{t}_{0}$ commute, we can combine the exponentials:\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into line $\\text{L7}$ gives line $\\text{L8}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L8}$ follows from $\\text{L7}$ by $\\text{the fact that }{e}^{X}{e}^{Y}={e}^{X+Y}\\text{ when }X\\text{ and }Y\\text{ commute}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7f8b56f9-e657-5fae-8f23-51af234be7a5"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0f7256d-2d29-5c85-9d4b-72a842efcbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\cos(6(t-3))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482307}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482307} :answer-field/id #uuid "199ebf62-577b-5ccf-9a87-eb53b5ca9e94" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1cf21ed-005b-59e4-9bd4-7e59ab89d886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sin(6(t-3))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482236} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cf580ea-9ff4-59fc-a20f-31f6a8ae4bf6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5cac624-fcaa-5546-985b-94ad2df95205" :question/math-academy-id "q-335436" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(3) \\\\ y(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "% Explanation\nWriting our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-3)}\\,x(3) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6(t-3))\\, \\\\ \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6(t-3))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0d055da-4dd2-51f2-ae55-dae45284bf27" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229684 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59d8cb4f-ee06-5141-9f71-0005f275d41d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481984} {:answer/id #uuid "65ee1f50-cf6b-58b8-afd8-663783704016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482010} {:answer/id #uuid "7e285e86-01d4-53c2-bae8-84c094816e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482063} {:answer/id #uuid "d4207d80-a6eb-5165-8ab9-aace8ee2daa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482281} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c4b7e3-8500-59d1-966d-52216ee1c994" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482291}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482291} :answer-field/id #uuid "283ab639-7505-5578-b135-707cc7103815" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8b9edfd9-1c2b-58af-aab6-8f8f25829073" :question/math-academy-id "q-335447" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(3) \\\\ y(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-3)}\\,x(3) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-3)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-3))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c1447d08-16ad-539a-8ebf-f17fc52a738b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482629}}] :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs Using Matrix Methods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481767}]}
